  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 229 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAs the shortwave over the west moves east on Thursday, it will\ncross through the CWA while merging into trough associated with\nthe closed low currently to the north. This will allow for a\n20-40% chance of showers over the area Thursday afternoon. As\nthe trough moves east, it will bring a cold front through the\narea, with cooler conditions and greater low level moisture\nbehind the front. This will be an ideal drizzle setup across the\narea Thursday night into Friday, with moisture trapped below an\nincreasingly sharp inversion. The big question is how long the\ndrizzle and/or low clouds last on Friday, with some guidance\nkeeping it around through much of the day. On top of drizzle\nremaining, this would result in cooler high temperatures than \ncurrently predicted and possibly not getting out of the 40s in\nsome areas. \n\nIf skies can clear, which most guidance suggests they will,\nFriday night will see lows in the upper 20s to mid 30s (coldest\nover the typical cold spots in the eastern Ozarks). Similar lows\nare expected through at least Sunday night and possibly into\nMonday night, resulting in widespread frost/freeze conditions.\nThere is high confidence that this forecast will remain on\ntrack, which would require headlines in the coming shifts. If\nthe clouds and drizzle can persist into Friday night,\ntemperatures would stay warmer due to the cloud cover and\nfreezing drizzle would not be expected.\n\nThe pattern becomes quite uncertain this weekend into next week\nas models show a wide range of outcomes with upper level energy.\nAt this time, we are not seeing any noteworthy precip chances\nfor this time period, but changes in temperature forecasts may\noccur as the picture becomes more clear.",
